# rfc7606-relay-one-field -- a relayed UPDATE carries one NLRI-bearing field
#
#
# RFC requirement: RFC7606-5.1-2 positive -- a received UPDATE mixing Withdrawn Routes with
# NLRI is SPLIT before being relayed, so every UPDATE this daemon puts on the wire carries at
# most one NLRI-bearing field. The unit tests pin the two splitters; this is the whole-daemon
# proof, wire-receive through the route server to the wire again.
# RFC requirement: RFC7606-5.1-3 positive -- the mixed shape is ACCEPTED on receive rather than
# answered with a NOTIFICATION (reject=stderr:pattern=notification at the foot of this file,
# plus the source peer staying up long enough for the forward to complete).
#
# THAT REJECT RULE WAS VACUOUS UNTIL 2026-07-29, and the gap was in the DAEMON, not here.
# The only route from a refused UPDATE to a NOTIFICATION is rfc7606SessionReset
# (reactor/session_validation.go:325) -> logNotifyErr -> sendNotification, and a SUCCESSFUL
# send logged NOTHING: it incremented a counter, a Prometheus label and a report-bus entry,
# none of which reach stderr. Injecting a forbidden rejection into enforceRFC7606 left the
# reject rule with no line to match at all. The fix was to give the daemon the log line an
# operator was already missing -- session_write.go now logs `notification sent` at WARN with
# peer/code/subcode -- not to retune the assertion to whatever the code happened to print.
#
# BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THAT BOUGHT. Re-running the same mutation now, ze DOES emit
# `level=WARN msg="notification sent" ... code="UPDATE Message Error" subcode=1`, so the rule
# has a real signal to match instead of matching nothing by construction. It does NOT make this
# mutation fail faster: that mutation also stops the forward, so the conn=2 expectation below
# times out at 15s and the runner reports the timeout first. What the rule uniquely catches is
# the case the expectations CANNOT: ze forwarding the split correctly AND still answering the
# source with a NOTIFICATION. Before the daemon fix, that case passed silently.
#
# THE BARE WORD WAS NOT SAFE AGAINST A FALSE RED, and this file used to claim it was on the
# strength of a SIGTERM measurement. Session.teardown (session_connection.go) sends
# Cease/AdminShutdown through logNotifyErr on the shutdown this file's observer asks for, and
# when the check-mode peer has already closed its socket that logs `notification send failed
# ... broken pipe`. Review round 5 of spec-fixit-peer-process-event-filter measured the file at
# 3 of 4 with steps 1 to 3 green, which is that line and nothing else. The reject rule at the
# foot of this file is now scoped to the NOTIFICATION code, so ze's own shutdown cannot trip
# it. (Whether ze SHOULD send that Cease is a separate question, unexamined here.)
#
# WHICH BRANCH THIS PROVES. buildFwdBody (reactor/forward_body.go:37) forks on `sameCtx` at :51,
# and the two arms compute the same verdict from different types. This file drives the PARSED
# arm (:74-112, verdict at :101 via message.Update.MixesNLRIFields): the two peers negotiate
# different encoding contexts, so the relay re-encodes rather than forwarding raw bytes.
# Verified by mutation, not by reading: disabling wireu.WireUpdate.MixesNLRIFields leaves this
# file GREEN, disabling message.Update.MixesNLRIFields turns it RED. The zero-copy arm at :60 is
# never reached here; it is covered by the unit test TestForwardSplitsMixedShapeSameContextThatFits
# (reactor/rfc7606_forward_body_test.go:53), which builds a same-context WireUpdate directly and
# carries the same RFC7606-5.1-2 tag. Read "whole-daemon proof" above as "of the parsed arm".
#
# THE 5.1-2 BINDING IS .ci-ONLY. Interop scenario 47 drives the same §5.1 shape through a real
# FRR and, since 2026-07-29, carries the 5.1-3 tag as a SECOND, nightly-tier binding -- accept-
# on-receive is exactly what a foreign peer can corroborate. It does not and cannot carry 5.1-2
# (spec-rfcgate-2-evidence AC-18, umbrella D3). Two reasons, and the second is the load-bearing
# one:
#   1. a .ci runs inside `./le verify current mode full` on every push; interop is nightly and advisory.
#   2. scenario 47 CANNOT discriminate the split. §5.1's third bullet obliges every receiver to
#      accept any combination of those fields, so a conforming FRR installs the route whether Ze
#      splits the UPDATE or relays it whole -- the scenario's own docstring says so. Only an
#      assertion over the exact bytes Ze emits can tell the two apart, which is what this file
#      does at conn=2 seq=1.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-1 -- a mixed-shape UPDATE received from a route-server client is split before
# it is relayed, so each UPDATE the receiver sees carries at most one of the four fields named
# by RFC 7606 Section 5.1's second bullet: "An UPDATE message MUST NOT contain more than one
# of the following: non-empty Withdrawn Routes field, non-empty Network Layer Reachability
# Information field, MP_REACH_NLRI attribute, and MP_UNREACH_NLRI attribute."
#
# VALIDATES: AC-3 -- the mixed shape is still ACCEPTED on receive. Section 5.1 also requires
# that an implementation "MUST still be prepared to receive these fields in any position or
# combination", so the source peer must not be reset. A NOTIFICATION or a dropped session
# would fail this test at the exit code.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-6 -- the withdrawal reaches the wire BEFORE the announcement. A peer must not
# briefly see a stale route re-announced, which is why wireu/split.go calls that order
# load-bearing and emits IPv4 withdrawals first by construction (buildCombinedUpdates).
#
# This header used to say AC-6 was NOT asserted, on the grounds that Checker.check matches an
# arriving message against every pending rule rather than the head, so declaration order is not
# arrival order. That is true only WITHIN one seq group. groupMessages
# (internal/test/peer/checker.go) builds one group per (conn, seq) and sorts them, and
# advanceSequence makes only the CURRENT group's rules live, so the seq=2 announce rule below
# cannot be consumed while the seq=1 withdrawal rule is still pending: an announcement arriving
# first matches nothing and fails as a mismatch. The two rules are in different seq groups, so
# this file HAS been asserting arrival order all along.
#
# Verified rather than reasoned: reversing result.rawBodies/result.updates at the end of
# buildFwdBody (reactor/forward_body.go), after supersedeKey and the withdrawal flag are
# computed so only emission order changes, fails this test 3/3 with "message mismatch" and the
# announcement as the only frame conn=2 accepted. The unit tests
# (TestForwardSplitMixedShapeWithdrawalsFirst, TestSplitCompliantWithdrawalsPrecedeAnnouncements)
# pin the two splitters; this file pins the whole daemon path.
#
# PREVENTS: the relay reproducing a received mixed shape. The unit tests pin buildFwdBody and
# both splitters directly; this test is the one that proves the behavior survives the whole
# daemon path, from wire receive through the route server to the wire again.
#
# Two eBGP peers, following bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci: source (65001) sends ONE UPDATE that
# both withdraws 172.16.0.0/24 and announces 10.0.0.0/24; receiver (65002) must see those as
# two separate UPDATEs.
#
# Deliberately WITHOUT `rs-fast-path enable`, unlike bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci. Both settings
# reach the code under test (buildFwdBody is on both routes), but with the fast path enabled
# the receiver intermittently also gets a SECOND announce of 10.0.0.0/24 whose AS_PATH is
# [65001] -- no 65000 prepend and no ATTR_253 -- which matches no rule and fails the run.
# Measured: 12/16 passes with it, 20/20 without, and 4/4 mutant failures either way. The
# extra frame is not caused by this change (bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci itself failed 1 in 6 on
# an unmodified tree, same EOR-versus-forward signature), so it is left to its own
# investigation rather than pinned here. What produces it is NOT established: the wire bytes
# are evidence, the producing function has not been read. An unprepended AS_PATH toward an
# eBGP peer would be a defect in its own right and is worth that investigation.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=timeout:value=15s
option=bind:value=0.0.0.0
# Map conn=N to a peer by remote IP instead of accept order (peer_connmap.go), and handshake
# both peers before either script runs, so the forward below is not a race. Same reasoning as
# bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci.
option=conn_map:value=remote-ip
# Wait for ze's End-of-RIB on BOTH connections before either script sends.
#
# The handshake alone is NOT the barrier this file's comment above assumed. It
# proves ze left OpenSent; it does not prove bgp-rs has registered the peer as a
# forward target. bgp-rs registers at the state=up event and captures its peer-up
# cut there, so a source UPDATE arriving before conn=2 is registered lands at or
# below that peer's cut and belongs to the Adj-RIB-In REPLAY -- which carries
# announcements only. The receiver then sees the announce and never the
# withdrawal this test asserts, which is exactly the intermittent failure above.
# ze emits the EoR only after that peer's initial sync, i.e. strictly after
# registration, so it is the first frame that proves the peer is a live target.
#
# Opt-in because consuming the EoR is only safe here: six of the nine conn_map
# tests declare it as an expectation and Checker.consumeMatches would be robbed
# of a frame they assert. This file declares none.
option=await_eor:value=true

# Source peer (conn=1): ONE UPDATE mixing Withdrawn Routes with NLRI.
#   withdrawn: 0004 18AC1000            -- 172.16.0.0/24
#   attrs:     001B ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH=[65001], NEXT_HOP=1.1.1.1, LOCAL_PREF=100
#   nlri:      180A0000                 -- 10.0.0.0/24
# Two NLRI-bearing fields in one message: exactly what Section 5.1 forbids a sender to emit,
# and exactly what an older speaker is allowed to send us.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF003A02000418AC1000001B4001010040020602010000FDE94003040101010140050400000064180A0000

# // NEITHER peer declares an End-of-RIB expectation, and that is deliberate
# rather than an omission. Checker.check (internal/test/peer/checker.go:414-418) silently
# accepts an unmatched KEEPALIVE or EOR, precisely because initial route sync races the read
# loop. Declaring the EOR opts INTO that race instead of out of it: whichever of the EOR and
# the forwarded UPDATE arrives first meets the rule, and this test failed twice that way on an
# unchanged tree (once at the source, once at the receiver) before the rules were left
# undeclared. Not declaring them costs no coverage here: neither EOR is part of the RFC 7606
# shape behavior under test, and conn=1's action=send is what keeps that peer listening.

# Receiver peer (conn=2), seq=1: the withdrawal, ALONE, asserted byte for byte.
#   0004 18AC1000 0000  -- withdrawn 172.16.0.0/24, no attributes, no NLRI
# This is the load-bearing assertion. A withdraw-only UPDATE on this wire can only exist if
# the relay split the received message: before this change the withdrawal was relayed inside
# the same UPDATE as the announcement, which cannot match these bytes. Full-message equality
# is used rather than contains= so that a message carrying the withdrawal PLUS anything else
# fails.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001B02000418AC10000000

# Receiver peer (conn=2), seq=2: the announcement, in its own UPDATE.
# The asserted bytes are the forwarded route's AS_PATH and NEXT_HOP:
#   AS_PATH:  40020A02020000FDE80000FDE9 (AS_SEQUENCE[65000, 65001] -- ze prepended its AS)
#   NEXT_HOP: 40030401010101 (1.1.1.1)
# contains= rather than hex= for the same reason bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci gives: the eBGP
# LOCAL_PREF rewrite is an open question settled elsewhere. These bytes cannot be satisfied by
# a withdraw, which carries no path attributes at all.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:contains=40020A02020000FDE80000FDE940030401010101
EOF_PEER

# Observer: shut down cleanly after forwarding completes.

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	internal adj-rib-in {
		use bgp-adj-rib-in
	}
	external shutdown-after-up {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/rfc7606-relay-one-field"
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process shutdown-after-up {
			receive [ update ]
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process shutdown-after-up {
			receive [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
# The source must not be reset for sending a mixed shape: Section 5.1 requires receivers to
# accept these fields in any combination.
#
# The reject pattern is scoped by CODE rather than by the word `notification`. The
# bare word also matched the daemon's OWN shutdown: Session.teardown
# (reactor/session_connection.go) sends Cease/AdminShutdown through logNotifyErr,
# which logs `notification send failed ... broken pipe` once the check-mode peer
# has closed. That is ze ending its own session, not ze refusing an UPDATE, and it
# failed this file 1 run in 4 under load. The line the pattern must catch is the
# one ze logs when it ANSWERS a message with an error: every such code renders
# through NotifyErrorCode.String (bgp/message/notification.go) as a name ending in
# `Error` -- Message Header Error, OPEN Message Error, UPDATE Message Error, Route
# Refresh Error, FSM Error -- and slog quotes it because it carries spaces.
# `Cease` is the one code that is NOT an answer to a message: it is how a speaker
# ends a session it chose to end, which is exactly ze's own shutdown. Nothing this
# file exists to catch is lost: rfc7606SessionReset (session_validation.go) is the
# only route from a refused UPDATE to a NOTIFICATION and it always passes
# message.NotifyUpdateMessage. `send failed` is kept as well, because ze ANSWERING
# with a NOTIFICATION violates Section 5.1 whether or not the write reached the
# peer.
#
# Measured, not reasoned. Rejecting the mixed shape at the head of enforceRFC7606 makes ze emit
#   level=WARN msg="notification sent" ... peer=127.0.0.1 code="UPDATE Message Error" subcode=1
# which this pattern matches; the mutation was reverted after the line was captured.
reject=stderr:pattern=notification (sent|send failed).*code="[^"]*Error"
